@database "llm_elevenlabs"
@author "Enrico Ehrich"
@$VER: llm_elevenlabs.guide 1.0 (16.10.2026)
@smartwrap

@node main "ElevenLabs text to speech"
@{h1}ElevenLabs text to speech

This plugin gives the TTS seam one more provider, @{tt}elevenlabs@{utt}:
ElevenLabs' text to speech over plain httpx, no SDK. It has no tools, no
hooks and no panel. You use it by naming it in a TTS model entry of
@{tt}config/llm.yaml@{utt} and pointing a TTS profile at that entry.

@{code}
  @{"  Model entry keys  " link keys}
  @{"  Voices and clones  " link voices}
  @{"  Pronunciation  " link ipa}
  @{"  Hooks, cost and errors  " link errors}
@{body}
@endnode

@node keys "Model entry keys"
@{h1}Model entry keys

@{table}
Key | Meaning
provider | elevenlabs
model | the model id, e.g. eleven_v4 (the default) or eleven_multilingual_v2
api_key | the account's key; empty falls back to ELEVENLABS_API_KEY
base_url | defaults to https://api.elevenlabs.io
voice | a voice id of the account, used when a call names none
request_timeout | seconds per request (300)
max_retries | retries on 429/5xx and transport errors (3)
@{body}

The audio comes back as 16-bit mono PCM at 24 kHz, a format every plan has.
@endnode

@node voices "Voices and clones"
@{h1}Voices and clones

A voice is an id of the account (Voices, ID). A caller passes it as the
voice's name, or the entry's @{tt}voice@{utt} applies.

A voice with a reference sample is cloned: the sample goes to
@{tt}POST /v1/voices/add@{utt} once, and the voice id it gets is kept in
@{tt}data/cache/elevenlabs_clones.json@{utt}, keyed by the sample's
SHA-256. Every later call with the same sample, in any process, speaks with
that id -- a book does not create a voice per scene, each would take a slot
of the account. If the account answers 404 for a cached id (the voice was
deleted), the entry is dropped and the sample cloned again, once. A preset
id that does not exist is an error, never a clone.
@endnode

@node ipa "Pronunciation"
@{h1}Pronunciation

@{tt}eleven_v4@{utt} reads IPA in slashes inside a line, also in German
text: "Er traf /ʃɪˈvɔn/." The client says so with
@{tt}supports_inline_ipa@{utt} (true for model ids starting with
@{tt}eleven_v4@{utt}). A caller with a pronunciation dictionary can read it
and send only the entries whose spoken form is IPA; respelled entries tend to
come out worse.
@endnode

@node errors "Hooks, cost and errors"
@{h1}Hooks, cost and errors

Each request fires the TTS hooks (provider @{tt}elevenlabs@{utt}), so the
message debugger sees it; the payload carries the text, model, voice label
and character count. No usage is reported: ElevenLabs bills characters and
its answer says nothing about them.

A 4xx answer is raised with the body's first 500 characters; 429 and 5xx are
retried with a growing pause. A call without any voice fails before a
request is sent.
@endnode
